WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.100
<v Maya>Welcome to part three.

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:05.320
<v Maya>Last time we built a weight training day in six steps.

3
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:12.650
<v Maya>Today we finish micro programming with the cardio day and a combined day, then the seven fundamentals and the four pillars.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.290
<v Dev>Starting with cardio.

5
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.610
<v Dev>Do we really need a warm-up when it is just cardio?

6
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:18.500
<v Maya>Yes.

7
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.990
<v Maya>The central nervous system still needs stimulating and the joints are still used.

8
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.120
<v Maya>So steps one and two stay the same, general warm-up and mobility drill.

9
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:33.340
<v Maya>Then steps three, four and five are three phases, which you know from the heart rate graphs in the cardio lecture.

10
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.800
<v Maya>Heart rate on the vertical axis, time along the bottom.

11
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.890
<v Dev>What does the graph look like?

12
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.870
<v Maya>Phase one is an upward slope.

13
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.480
<v Maya>Phase two is the main phase, and it looks different for each method.

14
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.690
<v Maya>Phase three is a downward slope.

15
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.880
<v Maya>For continuous cardio, phase two is a flat line.

16
00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:53.760
<v Maya>For low and moderate intensity intervals it looks like buildings.

17
00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:59.400
<v Maya>For HIIT, high intensity interval training, it looks like mountains with sharp peaks.

18
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.420
<v Dev>And what are the phases called?

19
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.480
<v Maya>Phase one is the re-warm-up.

20
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.350
<v Maya>Re, because you already did a general warm-up.

21
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:13.030
<v Maya>This one has a different purpose, to take the heart rate step by step into the target heart rate zone, about five minutes.

22
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.470
<v Maya>Phase two is the main or work phase.

23
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.590
<v Maya>Phase three is the cool-down.

24
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.080
<v Dev>Why cool down?

25
00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:20.780
<v Dev>Why not just stop?

26
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:26.370
<v Maya>Say it is the twentieth minute and the heart rate is one hundred and fifty to one hundred and sixty.

27
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.160
<v Maya>Stopping suddenly can cause dizziness, even a blackout, which is called syncope.

28
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.190
<v Maya>So bring it down step by step.

29
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.600
<v Maya>And step six is static passive stretching, because the muscles still worked.

30
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.840
<v Dev>So, what if a client wants weights and cardio on the same day?

31
00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.160
<v Maya>Weight training first, then cardio.

32
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:49.820
<v Maya>If cardio comes first, the muscles are already fatigued and cannot produce force in the main sets.

33
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:57.150
<v Maya>In the weight part do steps one to five and skip the static stretching, because you do not want to relax the muscles before cardio.

34
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.770
<v Maya>In the cardio part, skip the general warm-up and mobility drill, since the body is already warm.

35
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.960
<v Maya>Keep the re-warm-up, because it is specific to cardio.

36
00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:09.350
<v Maya>Then the main phase, then the cool-down, which is mandatory.

37
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.130
<v Maya>And do static stretching once, at the very end, never in the middle.

38
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.110
<v Dev>What about ten minutes of treadmill before lifting, as a warm-up?

39
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.510
<v Maya>Not recommended.

40
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.670
<v Maya>It fatigues the muscles beforehand.

41
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.170
<v Maya>The general warm-up is just a slow two to three minute walk.

42
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.370
<v Maya>Pause and think.

43
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.800
<v Maya>On a combined day, which stretching step is skipped after the weights, and why?

44
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:35.120
<v Dev>The static stretch, because relaxing the muscles before cardio is not what we want.

45
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.430
<v Dev>We do it once at the very end.

46
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.570
<v Maya>Exactly.

47
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.430
<v Maya>And here is an exam hint.

48
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:47.100
<v Maya>A multiple choice question is very likely, asking for the most logical micro programme, with the steps jumbled.

49
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.720
<v Maya>Static stretching first, mobility drill last, and so on.

50
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.420
<v Maya>If you know the three days, you can answer any version.

51
00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.570
<v Maya>Next aisle.

52
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.300
<v Maya>The fundamentals of exercise programming.

53
00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.650
<v Maya>Seven of them, equally important.

54
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:00.920
<v Maya>Not a ranking.

55
00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:06.750
<v Maya>Specificity, adaptation, overload, progression, intensity, duration, reversibility.

56
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.600
<v Dev>Let's go one by one.

57
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:09.920
<v Maya>Specificity.

58
00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.570
<v Maya>A specific goal needs a specific task.

59
00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:19.160
<v Maya>If the goal is lower body strength and the client must avoid back stress, choose a supported lower body exercise.

60
00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.610
<v Maya>Like an employee who wants a promotion for learning a new tool.

61
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.040
<v Maya>They learn that tool.

62
00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.090
<v Maya>They do not join a dance class.

63
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.560
<v Dev>Adaptation?

64
00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.070
<v Maya>Add a new movement and the body adapts to it, if you keep doing it.

65
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.460
<v Maya>Someone who has never done a push-up manages two or three.

66
00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.690
<v Maya>If they continue, after a month they may do fifteen.

67
00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.060
<v Dev>Overload.

68
00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.290
<v Maya>If the weight never increases, tension inside the muscle does not increase.

69
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.100
<v Maya>The mechanoreceptors, which inform the nervous system about tension, stay quiet.

70
00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.920
<v Maya>The current myofibrils and sarcomeres are enough, so why should the muscle grow?

71
00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:55.710
<v Maya>So after adaptation, you must overload.

72
00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.480
<v Dev>Progression is next.

73
00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:02.780
<v Maya>Progression is what you see when specificity, adaptation and overload work together.

74
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:06.960
<v Maya>Intensity and duration are taught in detail in the next section.

75
00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.700
<v Maya>Duration is most relevant for cardio but also varies for weight training.

76
00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.650
<v Dev>Then reversibility.

77
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:20.510
<v Maya>Any achievement, fat loss or hypertrophy, goes back if you stop training, cheat the diet, or ignore recovery.

78
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.870
<v Maya>And growth is slower than the downfall.

79
00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.310
<v Maya>In a study example, a hundred people trained for three months and gained muscle.

80
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.320
<v Maya>When some stopped training, the muscle came off much faster than it was built.

81
00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.760
<v Maya>The lecture used hypothetical numbers, so do not quote them as research.

82
00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.220
<v Dev>Is the speed the same for everyone?

83
00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:39.060
<v Maya>No.

84
00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:46.740
<v Maya>It is slower for someone with prior training experience, because the neural pathways and proprioception are already trained.

85
00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:51.010
<v Maya>A six year lifter on a six month break loses less quickly than a newbie.

86
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.400
<v Maya>And nutrition, especially protein, affects the pace.

87
00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.290
<v Dev>A mnemonic for the seven?

88
00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.700
<v Maya>Say it as a story.

89
00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:05.420
<v Maya>A specific task, the body adapts, you overload it, you progress, at the right intensity and duration, and if you stop, it reverses.

90
00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.760
<v Maya>Now the four pillars of exercise programming, like the pillars of a house.

91
00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.520
<v Maya>The lecture calls it the FIT principle.

92
00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.760
<v Maya>The slides call it FITT.

93
00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.260
<v Maya>Same four pillars.

94
00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.900
<v Maya>Frequency, intensity, time and type.

95
00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:22.230
<v Maya>Each is applied to weight training, cardio and stretching.

96
00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.920
<v Dev>And F is frequency, not fitness.

97
00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:25.820
<v Maya>Right.

98
00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.930
<v Maya>Let's learn to read a frequency chart, like a client's existing diary.

99
00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:38.030
<v Maya>Monday upper body weight training, Tuesday lower body, Wednesday cardio, Thursday upper, Friday lower, Saturday cardio, Sunday rest.

100
00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.510
<v Maya>Read it in layers.

101
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:40.830
<v Dev>Layer one?

102
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.280
<v Maya>Type of activity.

103
00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.010
<v Maya>Weight training four times, cardio twice.

104
00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.190
<v Maya>Layer two, by body region.

105
00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.890
<v Maya>Upper body twice and lower body twice.

106
00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.000
<v Maya>Layer three, by single muscle.

107
00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:55.850
<v Maya>The latissimus dorsi is trained twice, Monday and Thursday.

108
00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:59.360
<v Maya>Not four, because two of the four sessions were leg days.

109
00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:00.940
<v Dev>And going deeper?

110
00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.300
<v Maya>Parts of the muscle.

111
00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.420
<v Maya>The lats have horizontal and diagonal fibres.

112
00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:10.180
<v Maya>Shoulder extension exercises, like rowing, target the horizontal fibres.

113
00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:15.090
<v Maya>Shoulder adduction exercises, like pulldowns, target the diagonal fibres.

114
00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.710
<v Maya>So you can say how often each is trained.

115
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.690
<v Dev>What about synergists?

116
00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:20.850
<v Maya>Important.

117
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:26.200
<v Maya>The triceps work as synergists in the bench press, in overhead pressing and in chest work.

118
00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:31.490
<v Maya>If you only look for a triceps exercise on the chart, you miss them and end up overtraining.

119
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.470
<v Dev>And cardio and stretching?

120
00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.120
<v Maya>Cardio is classified by joint stress.

121
00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:40.220
<v Maya>An outdoor run is WBIB, weight bearing impact based.

122
00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:54.100
<v Maya>Indoors you may choose WBIB on a treadmill, WBNIB, weight bearing non impact based, on an elliptical, or NWBNIB, non weight bearing non impact based, on a bike, rowing machine or swimming.

123
00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:57.100
<v Maya>For stretching, the example has six sessions a week.

124
00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:02.700
<v Maya>Mobility drill six, static six, for example four SPS and two PNF.

125
00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:10.010
<v Maya>PNF, proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation, is high intensity, so at most twice a week.

126
00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.220
<v Maya>Stretching does not follow the forty eight hour rule.

127
00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:14.610
<v Dev>Intensity?

128
00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.180
<v Maya>Weight training uses the RM method.

129
00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.950
<v Maya>The lower the RM, the higher the intensity.

130
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.270
<v Maya>Also RIR, reps in reserve.

131
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.660
<v Maya>Fewer in reserve, higher intensity.

132
00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.490
<v Maya>Zero or one in reserve is close to failure.

133
00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.350
<v Maya>Four or five in reserve is low intensity.

134
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:31.850
<v Dev>And cardio?

135
00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:38.750
<v Maya>Heart rate during cardio as a percentage of maximum heart rate, with the target worked out by the Karvonen formula.

136
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:51.710
<v Maya>When heart rate cannot be used, for patients or pregnant women, use RPE, the Borg rating of perceived exertion from zero to ten, where nine is really, really hard and ten is maximum, or the talk test.

137
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:57.720
<v Maya>Stretching, static passive is lower intensity, and PNF and isometric stretching are high.

138
00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:58.920
<v Maya>Pause and think.

139
00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:05.600
<v Maya>A client's plan has one triceps exercise a week, but also a bench press and overhead press on other days.

140
00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.710
<v Maya>Is triceps frequency one?

141
00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:08.610
<v Dev>No.

142
00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:12.430
<v Dev>The triceps work as synergists, so it is more than one.

143
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.570
<v Maya>Exactly.

144
00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:14.630
<v Maya>Now time.

145
00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:22.400
<v Maya>For weight training, beginners have no three minute rest because they have no main sets, but ten to fifteen reps, so the session is still long.

146
00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:26.390
<v Maya>Intermediate and advanced have rests but only four to six reps.

147
00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:30.910
<v Maya>Cardio is continuous, aerobic, for more than three minutes, even hours.

148
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.300
<v Maya>Or interval, short and anaerobic.

149
00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:36.180
<v Maya>Static stretching is time based, thirty seconds.

150
00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.910
<v Maya>Dynamic is repetition based, ten to twelve reps per drill.

151
00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:45.590
<v Maya>The slides instead say mobility drills for five to ten minutes in total, and both can be true.

152
00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.550
<v Dev>Is there a calculation?

153
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.330
<v Maya>A HIIT session.

154
00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.070
<v Maya>Five minutes re-warm-up.

155
00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.270
<v Maya>Three sprints of fifteen seconds is forty five seconds.

156
00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:57.830
<v Maya>Two active recoveries of one and a half minutes is three minutes.

157
00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.020
<v Maya>So the work phase is three minutes forty five seconds.

158
00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:02.880
<v Maya>Add five minutes cool-down.

159
00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:07.700
<v Maya>Five plus three forty five plus five is thirteen minutes forty five seconds.

160
00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:13.570
<v Maya>The recording once mentioned four sprints, but the calculation used three, so check your course material.

161
00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:14.820
<v Dev>And type?

162
00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:16.040
<v Maya>Weight training.

163
00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:19.930
<v Maya>Compound versus isolation, multiple joints versus one.

164
00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.040
<v Maya>Supported versus unsupported.

165
00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.860
<v Maya>Then bilateral, both sides same object.

166
00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:27.870
<v Maya>Isolateral, both sides different objects.

167
00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.280
<v Maya>Unilateral, one side at a time.

168
00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:37.130
<v Maya>A structural lift is bilateral, compound, unsupported, with axial loading, involving both knee and hip.

169
00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:47.600
<v Maya>Cardio is WBIB, WBNIB, NWBNIB, and continuous or interval, like LIIT, MIIT, HIIT and Fartlek.

170
00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:55.440
<v Maya>Stretching is dynamic or static, where static includes SPS, isometric, PNF and fascial line stretching.

171
00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.030
<v Dev>And S and V?

172
00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.930
<v Maya>The finishing touches, like interior decoration.

173
00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:01.310
<v Maya>S is sequence.

174
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:06.330
<v Maya>Unsupported compound, supported compound, isolation, then unilateral.

175
00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.750
<v Maya>V is volume, sets times reps.

176
00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.760
<v Maya>Reps are four to six RM.

177
00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.400
<v Maya>Sets come next lecture.

178
00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.310
<v Maya>The slides add guidelines.

179
00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.710
<v Maya>Stretching six times a week, cardio three to five, weight training three to four.

180
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.150
<v Maya>Cardio twenty to sixty minutes, weight training twenty to ninety.

181
00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.820
<v Maya>Five things to remember.

182
00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:30.760
<v Maya>One, a cardio day has a general warm-up, mobility drill, re-warm-up, main phase, cool-down and static stretching.

183
00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.330
<v Dev>Two, on a combined day, weights first.

184
00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:38.190
<v Dev>Skip the static stretch in the middle and skip the general warm-up before cardio.

185
00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.410
<v Dev>One static stretch at the end.

186
00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:42.480
<v Maya>Three, the seven fundamentals.

187
00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:50.550
<v Maya>Specificity, adaptation, overload, progression, intensity, duration, reversibility, and growth is slower than the downfall.

188
00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.310
<v Dev>Four, the four pillars.

189
00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:57.620
<v Dev>Frequency, intensity, time, type, read in layers, including synergists.

190
00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:02.400
<v Dev>And the HIIT session lasts thirteen minutes forty five seconds.

191
00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:05.550
<v Maya>Five, sequence and volume are the finishing touches.

192
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.900
<v Maya>Now we are ready to cook in the next lecture.

